Morrissey has written an open letter to Queen Mary University of London (QMUL), calling on the institution to end its practice of deliberately inducing sepsis in animals.

The letter was made public on Thursday (November 26) by animal rights organisation PETA, who say the university has confirmed that it is not currently using a particular method of inducing sepsis in mice, but has not committed to ending all such experiments.

### 🎤 Key Highlights & Community Reactions

Addressing the university’s president Professor Colin Bailey in the letter , the former Smiths frontman wrote: “As I prepare to tour the UK, I am reminded that behind the grand facades of some of England’s more venerable institutions, unspeakable cruelty is inflicted in the name of science. Queen Mary University of London, I understand, is no exception.

“My friends at PETA tell me your university has a shameful history of deliberately inducing sepsis in mice. In these grotesque experiments, mice have been cut open and their intestines punctured, allowing faeces to leak into their abdomens. They can then endure the agonising effects of severe sepsis – including extreme suffering, fever, difficulty breathing and organ failure – before being killed. And for what, exactly?”

Morrissey went on to argue that the differences between mice and humans undermine the effectiveness of the research, citing PETA’s finding that more than 150 drugs that successfully treated sepsis in mice subsequently failed in humans.

Morrissey has a message for Queen Mary University of London: Stop making animals suffer in the name of science. 🐭💔

